  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Effective wage policies to promote fair and decent wages through improving minimum wages  and wage setting practices</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Fair and decent wages are integral part of sustainable business and workers&#x2019; welfare, and hence the effectiveness of (minimum) wage setting mechanisms play a key role in Decent Work.  In Indonesia, wages have been a central topic of collective bargaining and policy debate. 
At the same time, longer working hours have become more common as companies in Indonesia looked to become part of value chains. In 2020, the Omnibus Law amended regulations on working hours and rest, which extended the number of working hours that can be worked. This pressure to work longer hours can increase depending on the level of the minimum wage rates and overtime premia rates. There is growing pressures to address how to organize working hours and rest periods moving forward. Many more workers and employers had to learn how to implement and use telework and other digital platform, with varying results. Growing concerns around working hours and working arrangements and ways to structure these systems to meet worker and employer needs are important as organizations look to move forward from recent events. The ILO&#x2019;s technical assistance intends to facilitate effective wage policies and promote decent wages through improving the minimum wage system and wage setting practices at the company and the sector level. The ILO builds the capacity of tripartite constituents in monitoring wage trends vis-&#xE0;-vis socio-economic indicators and formulating appropriate wage policies based on social dialogue. It also enhances social partners&#x2019; capacity to negotiate and determine fair compensation and benefits. This outcome therefore prepares tripartite constituents to build fair wage setting mechanism.</narrative>
  </description>
